\begin{lemma}[A finite annular net uniform in circumference]
\label{lem:annular-finite-net}
\uses{def:annular-geodesic}
\lean{PoincareMT.M64FamilyAnnulusNet,PoincareMT.m64FamilyAnnulusNets_of_compact}
Let $\Gamma:S^2\to\Lambda^1M$ be continuous and let $\mu>0$.
There are finitely many $z_1,\ldots,z_m\in S^2$ and $\rho_*>0$
such that every $z$ has one selected $z_i$ for which, for every
$0<\rho<\rho_*$, an actual initial annulus joins their canonical
ramps and has area less than $\mu$. The node is selected before
$\rho$ and works at all smaller circumferences. Nullity is not required.
\source{MT2007}\dcref{pp. 461--462}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The compact family has a uniform angular derivative bound.
The minimizing interpolator has a uniform endpoint differential
bound $B$ near the diagonal. For uniformly $\epsilon$-close loops,
its canonical lift has column bounds $B+1,\epsilon$ for all
$0<\rho\leq2\pi$, hence area at most $2\pi(B+1)\epsilon$.
Choose $\epsilon$ to make this less than $\mu$ and then take a finite
cover of the compact family by uniform $\epsilon$-balls. The same
center and interpolator work for every smaller circumference.
\end{proof}

